A||||26||watts||Rear hood strut update||||4||
Z||000000||watts||12-03-2001||12:48 AM||||Hi people...<P>Those who were planning on dashing off to the store to buy a spanky new strut from an '84 Regal front hood, and slap it onto the back of there Fiero - Don't.<P>It's a REALLY nice fit!  Looks great.  Problem is, it takes over 300lbs of force to collapse the strut!  Your hood would snap in half long before it ever closed.  This is counter productive.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  And the intended mounting point sure won't take that kind of pressure.  <P>I'm sure you could get away with a nice old worn out one, but...<P><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||RedOktober||12-03-2001||03:26 AM||||Watts...are you referring to this post?  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012758.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012758.html</A> <P>cuz thats what I wanted to try. But when I looked at the bracket, it looks a little too thin to use to support a strut rod. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/red6[1]a.jpg">   <BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV "Red Oktober"<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater) "Blackie"<BR>87 GT "Blue Flame"<p>[This message has been edited by RedOktober (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Haze_Performance||12-03-2001||03:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by watts:<BR><B>Hi people...<P>Those who were planning on dashing off to the store to buy a spanky new strut from an '84 Regal front hood, and slap it onto the back of there Fiero - Don't.<P>It's a REALLY nice fit!  Looks great.  Problem is, it takes over 300lbs of force to collapse the strut!  Your hood would snap in half long before it ever closed.  This is counter productive.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  And the intended mounting point sure won't take that kind of pressure.  <P>I'm sure you could get away with a nice old worn out one, but...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Thanks for the update... I was going to do mine in a few weeks..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlife.gif">      <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="red">1984 Indy For Sale ~ <A HREF="http://www.ClassicFiero.com" TARGET=_blank>Click Here</A> </FONT></FONT>||||reg||7
Z||000003||falconhulk||12-03-2001||07:58 AM||||300 lbs????  That cant be right.  No hood or truk lid wieghs that much. Thats an insane amount of force.  Also I have seen maybe 10 post from people who have done this.  Not sure if all used the Buick one though||||reg||1
Z||000004||revin||12-03-2001||08:02 AM||||The old wooden stick still works! and cheap! ha ha <BR>my pathfinder has some worn out struts what size does the black part have to be for the Fiero? and how do you measure how much the deck weighs?||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||12-03-2001||09:20 AM||||That's why Rodney's strut kit is so good. He chooses the right pressure based on your decklid and what's on it.<P>And his kit goes on in about 15 minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000006||jimmybpei||12-03-2001||10:00 AM||||There must be a shock that would be adiquite to do the job. Just a matter of finding the proper one.||||reg||1
Z||000007||tesmith66||12-03-2001||11:04 AM||||300 lbs is the amount of force the strut can produce. The hood it was designed to support may only weigh 100 punds, but the force needed to support it at about a foot from the pivot is 300 lbs. <P>Does anyone know where to find all of the specs (length, diameter, force, etc) for these things? Maybe we can find one the same length, but with less force. Without doing any measuring or calculations, my shooting from the hip guess would be 50 - 75 lbs(?)<P>Any suggestions????<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||5
Z||000008||watts||12-03-2001||12:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by RedOktober:<BR><B>Watts...are you referring to this post?  [URL=http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012758.html][/URL] <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yup, that's the one.  Like I say - with a used strut, it might work, but who's to say how worn something is?<P>Falconhulk:  As for 300lbs of force being an insane amount of force.  We put one end of the strut on the shop floor.  Balanced a 2*4 across the top.  Combined weight of about 330lbs between my freind and myself on each end could only move it about half way down.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroJoe||12-03-2001||12:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by watts:<BR><B> Yup, that's the one.  Like I say - with a used strut, it might work, but who's to say how worn something is?<P>Falconhulk:  As for 300lbs of force being an insane amount of force.  We put one end of the strut on the shop floor.  Balanced a 2*4 across the top.  Combined weight of about 330lbs between my freind and myself on each end could only move it about half way down.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Remember, though, that when you pull on the edge of your decklid, you have a lever thats about a meter long, so it does have a little bit of mechanical advantage for compressing the strut.  But, yes, you're right, that is still a very tough strut and the mounting point isn't very strong.  After all, its only  made to hold a wiring harness.<P>I have installed Rodney's kits before, and they are top notch and worth the money!  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||SLYGUY||12-03-2001||12:57 PM||||I looked at the set up that Rodney has. Only $62 for the single strut and it bolts right up. <P>I'm feeling some serious heat from the ATM card in my wallet right about now!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000011||qwikgta||12-03-2001||02:10 PM||||Sorry, it never occured to me that the regal strut that I got from the boneyard was that worn.  It works great with a worn out old Buick Regal strut.  I guess I should have grabbed the other one too. <P>Rob <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sig_Pic_copy.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||jelly2m8||12-03-2001||05:01 PM||||using a 2x4 with 2 guys pressing on it, isn't much of a measurement... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>It is hard as hell to compress ANY of those hood / decklid struts using that method, or similar.<P> You forgot the point about leverage. take the springs off your decklid, and try lifting it up at the hinge. Good luck.<P><BR>I used the 84 Buick  Regal/ Century hood strut on my V8, and it was new BTW.<P>You can unlock / pop the deck lid, but it doesn't fly open, you still need to lift it up about half way before the strut will take over. To close it, just use 1 arm, and pull the decklid down, and I'm not very strong BTW.<P> So before you come in and mis-inform people make sure you get your facts straight.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||watts||12-03-2001||05:14 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B> So before you come in and mis-inform people make sure you get your facts straight.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Really?  Shall I post pictures of the mis-informed bracket that tore off of the sheet metal due to excess pressures?  I'm sure it would have stayed in place had it read your post first.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P><BR>Anyhow...   I made up a new bracket (see below) and found a strut that works.  If anyones interested in my uninformed opinions on the device that's in my car now and works fine I'll post more.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/deckstrut.JPG">  <P>BTW - can we see pics of your bracket where the strut is mounting?  Are you using Rodney's kit?  (which I don't believe uses the Regal strut).<P>BTW2 - I'm not forgetting about leverage when i say it takes 300 lbs of force to collapse the Regals strut.  The strut I used (that works) requires ABOUT 50-75lbs of force to close it.  This is not to say the hood weighs that much, or needs that much to lift it, or needs more.  I'm saying it works for me (incidently, my reason for needing the strut is that I added a power release and factory wing).  I can now open the hood, and it will stay at virtually any position I put it.<P>This definetly falls under the "YMMV" banner.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by watts (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||12-03-2001||05:20 PM||||tsmiths right, at the point the strut attaches, it may need 300 pnds to hold up a 100 pnd hood. the struts are usually located quite a bit back from the front edge. Its called leverage. take that buick hood and without struts try to hold the hood up a foot from the rear, it wont happen. All youll get is a severely broken hand.||||reg||1
Z||000015||tesmith66||12-03-2001||05:25 PM||||Watts, <P>What strut did you use?<P>Jelly2m8,<P>Does your car have a wing??<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe<p>[This message has been edited by tesmith66 (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||jelly2m8||12-03-2001||05:30 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by watts:<BR>[B] Really?  Shall I post pictures of the mis-informed bracket that tore off of the sheet metal due to excess pressures?  I'm sure it would have stayed in place had it read your post first.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Umm, DUH, why would you attach a hood strut to a piece of sheet metal?<P> By the looks of you latest bracket, no wonder you needed a weaker strut.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> OK, seriously now, yer strut looks good, not how I would have done it, but looks good, and probably works great.<P> BTW the previously mentioned strut does work good when properly supported. <P> <P>  <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/P8110011.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
